WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.089 --> 00:00:05.940
The disparity in quality going from Secret
Invasion to Loki Season 2 gave me insane whiplash.

00:00:05.940 --> 00:00:10.840
On one hand, we have a terribly executed mess
that had a crisis behind the scenes, and then

00:00:10.840 --> 00:00:15.860
on the other, we have a triumph in storytelling
that tells a singular and spectacular vision

00:00:15.860 --> 00:00:17.510
for a loveable character.

00:00:17.510 --> 00:00:22.050
Because that’s why Loki works so well over
so many of these other projects.

00:00:22.050 --> 00:00:26.670
It has a deeply engaging story that has a
thoroughly evolving main character.

00:00:26.670 --> 00:00:31.560
Loki at the start of the show and to the end
of season 2 are practically opposite characters

00:00:31.560 --> 00:00:35.700
and that kind of journey, written in a compelling
manner is amazing.

00:00:35.700 --> 00:00:39.630
This is going into what I think is by far
the best aspect of Loki season 2 which is

00:00:39.630 --> 00:00:44.590
Eric Martin’s writing of characters, their
journeys, and their internal conflicts.

00:00:44.590 --> 00:00:48.680
Everyone on the roster besides maybe one or
two characters have fully fleshed out arcs

00:00:48.680 --> 00:00:53.020
with tangible motivations that make them riveting
and likable characters.

00:00:53.020 --> 00:00:56.989
This is what I would identify as Marvel’s
major issue in that most character work has

00:00:56.989 --> 00:01:01.100
taken a back seat as they prioritize screen
time in other areas.

00:01:01.100 --> 00:01:04.960
Recent examples being in Ant-Man Quantumania
where Scott and Cassie’s relationship feels

00:01:04.960 --> 00:01:11.590
very shallow and in The Marvels, Carol Danvers’
primary internal conflict is wholly underdeveloped.

00:01:11.590 --> 00:01:15.151
Then when you look at Marvel’s recent great
movies like Guardians of the Galaxy 3 and

00:01:15.151 --> 00:01:20.180
No Way Home, they tell engrossing stories
that are primarily driven by their ever evolving

00:01:20.180 --> 00:01:21.180
characters.

00:01:21.180 --> 00:01:25.680
Loki Season 2 I think falls right into that
category of greatness because of how excellent

00:01:25.680 --> 00:01:27.180
its character work is.

00:01:27.180 --> 00:01:32.390
Loki’s villain to hero arc is a really hard
thing to pull off because it requires a complex

00:01:32.390 --> 00:01:34.800
dive into the psyche of his character.

00:01:34.800 --> 00:01:39.000
He goes from someone who desperately tried
to grasp at control and purpose in his life

00:01:39.000 --> 00:01:44.009
through any form of evil means, all the way
to someone who only accepts power out of sheer

00:01:44.009 --> 00:01:46.290
necessity of doing the right thing.

00:01:46.290 --> 00:01:50.369
And since this was done in a show setting,
it gave the creators enough time to fully

00:01:50.369 --> 00:01:53.420
explore this rich character arc to its full
potential.

00:01:53.420 --> 00:01:58.700
We actually have long and intriguing dialogue
scenes that are imbued with important revelations

00:01:58.700 --> 00:02:00.149
for our characters.

00:02:00.149 --> 00:02:06.090
Like most dialogue scenes between two characters
generally lead to one of them changing immensely.

00:02:06.090 --> 00:02:09.950
Examples being Loki sparking the interest
of what Mobious’ life was like outside of

00:02:09.950 --> 00:02:14.250
the TVA, Loki’s talk with Sylvia in which
she learns that purely destroying something

00:02:14.250 --> 00:02:19.540
want solve problems, and Mobius driving in
the final nail to Loki’s arc in that one’s

00:02:19.540 --> 00:02:24.379
purpose is primarily burden over glory, and
the burden is inevitable.

00:02:24.379 --> 00:02:28.860
Delivering Loki’s final revelation in that
he has to become a time god and be burdened

00:02:28.860 --> 00:02:31.280
with taking care of the entire multiverse.

00:02:31.280 --> 00:02:33.430
A truly selfless act.

00:02:33.430 --> 00:02:38.010
These are just a few examples, but every episode
is packed full of these incredible dialogue

00:02:38.010 --> 00:02:39.010
scenes.

00:02:39.010 --> 00:02:42.980
Now some are obviously better than others
and I’ll get into what I think are some

00:02:42.980 --> 00:02:46.840
of the show’s faults towards the latter
half of the video, but the batting average

00:02:46.840 --> 00:02:48.370
is still really high.

00:02:48.370 --> 00:02:51.900
So much to the point that it’s admirable
that primarily character and dialogue-driven

00:02:51.900 --> 00:02:54.730
scenes carry this show so much.

00:02:54.730 --> 00:02:59.409
Like looking at any other Marvel show, they
generally have to interject some kind of artificial

00:02:59.409 --> 00:03:01.840
action to make the episode more exciting.

00:03:01.840 --> 00:03:07.129
However, Loki doesn’t need to do this because
of how high-quality the drama is in the show.

00:03:07.129 --> 00:03:11.250
Easily the best and most refreshing climax
in any Marvel show is the showdown between

00:03:11.250 --> 00:03:13.629
He Who Remains and Loki and Sylvie.

00:03:13.629 --> 00:03:18.349
There’s no crazy spectacle or action setpiece
to entertain the audience and it’s purely

00:03:18.349 --> 00:03:19.349
dialogue.

00:03:19.349 --> 00:03:24.049
And Loki season 2 somehow elevates it and
adds an additional layer onto that finale

00:03:24.049 --> 00:03:25.170
from the first season.

00:03:25.170 --> 00:03:29.850
I honestly was kind of skeptical going into
the finale of Loki season 2, but it far and

00:03:29.850 --> 00:03:31.950
away exceeded my expectations.

00:03:31.950 --> 00:03:36.360
The second finale confrontation with He Who
Remains really reminds me of the final showdown

00:03:36.360 --> 00:03:38.620
between Sherlock Holmes and Moriarty.

00:03:38.620 --> 00:03:43.170
In that movie, they are playing these complex
mindgames where they are constantly countering

00:03:43.170 --> 00:03:44.170
each other.

00:03:44.170 --> 00:03:48.780
And with the season 2 finale, it reveals He
Who Remains’ true plan in that he knew Loki

00:03:48.780 --> 00:03:52.790
would end up coming back to stop Sylvie because
of the loom’s failsafe in destroying all

00:03:52.790 --> 00:03:53.860
of time.

00:03:53.860 --> 00:03:57.489
It actually makes He Who Remains a more evil
character because it seems like he did this

00:03:57.489 --> 00:03:59.060
out of pure boredom.

00:03:59.060 --> 00:04:03.090
He knew the outcome would inevitably end the
way it would and he’s doing this to torture

00:04:03.090 --> 00:04:07.590
Loki in that he now has to kill someone he
cares about to save all of time.

00:04:07.590 --> 00:04:12.560
However, like in Sherlock Holmes, this action
made by the villain that comes across as seemingly

00:04:12.560 --> 00:04:16.280
being a checkmate can actually be countered
by our hero.

00:04:16.280 --> 00:04:20.290
This is the kind of writing that is outstanding
in which the viewer doesn’t know a logical

00:04:20.290 --> 00:04:24.759
way in overcoming the conflict and the hero
has to come up with something that is clever.

00:04:24.759 --> 00:04:29.139
For Loki, his solution to He Who Remains’
conundrum isn’t just a clever solution,

00:04:29.139 --> 00:04:33.470
but it’s also an integral step for Loki
in finishing his character arc.

00:04:33.470 --> 00:04:38.450
It ironically acts as a lay-up of sorts for
Loki in finding his glorious purpose and finishing

00:04:38.450 --> 00:04:40.860
his ultimate redemption arc.

00:04:40.860 --> 00:04:45.600
Overcoming conflict is one thing, but if you
can also make it expertly tie into the main

00:04:45.600 --> 00:04:48.500
character’s arc, then that is great writing.

00:04:48.500 --> 00:04:50.570
Which is what Eric Martin achieves here.

00:04:50.570 --> 00:04:54.789
Along with the monumental task of creating
an hour worth of riveting dialogue which is

00:04:54.789 --> 00:04:56.750
essentially what the ending is.

00:04:56.750 --> 00:05:01.190
It just requires a lot more skill and artistry
to write something of this caliber over something

00:05:01.190 --> 00:05:06.639
like the Wandavision finale which feels significantly
more convoluted and messy narratively.

00:05:06.639 --> 00:05:09.780
And I’m being generous bringing that up
as a comparison because that’s one of the

00:05:09.780 --> 00:05:11.230
better ones.

00:05:11.230 --> 00:05:15.570
Also for the season 2 finale, I love how they
essentially turned half of the episode into

00:05:15.570 --> 00:05:19.690
Groundhogs Day which is one of my favorite
movies, but applied it to a hardcore science

00:05:19.690 --> 00:05:20.889
fiction setting.

00:05:20.889 --> 00:05:25.090
Seeing Loki ram his head into a wall trying
to overcome this mind-boggling conflict was

00:05:25.090 --> 00:05:26.440
incredibly engrossing.

00:05:26.440 --> 00:05:30.630
It would have been nice if the finale was
longer because I feel like the writers downplayed

00:05:30.630 --> 00:05:35.310
the mental impact of doing the same thing
over and over for centuries, but it was still

00:05:35.310 --> 00:05:36.630
well executed.

00:05:36.630 --> 00:05:41.040
Maybe Loki is more resilient to long stretches
of time because he is a god after all.

00:05:41.040 --> 00:05:45.480
So Loki’s character arc is obviously outstanding
and the other two main side characters, being

00:05:45.480 --> 00:05:48.350
Mobius and Sylvie are also great of course.

00:05:48.350 --> 00:05:52.590
Mobius and Loki’s dynamic is still very
endearing and the writers lean into that even

00:05:52.590 --> 00:05:54.030
more for season 2.

00:05:54.030 --> 00:05:58.280
At times it did feel a little forced, an example
being Mobius leaving during a fight scene

00:05:58.280 --> 00:06:02.410
to go find a bike with two seats for him and
Loki, while in other moments it felt more

00:06:02.410 --> 00:06:03.410
natural.

00:06:03.410 --> 00:06:07.280
As long as the writers weren’t going out
of their way for any shenanigans, I felt like

00:06:07.280 --> 00:06:08.280
it was fine.

00:06:08.280 --> 00:06:12.620
But for Mobius overall, I think it was pretty
genius in that the writers were able to showcase

00:06:12.620 --> 00:06:16.561
his entire life on the timeline while also
building Loki’s character in overcoming

00:06:16.561 --> 00:06:17.819
time slipping.

00:06:17.819 --> 00:06:22.319
It was very efficient and offered a significant
amount of value for Mobius as a character

00:06:22.319 --> 00:06:24.850
as we got to witness his entire backstory.

00:06:24.850 --> 00:06:27.980
And the same thing applies for the other more
minor side characters.

00:06:27.980 --> 00:06:33.630
Like up until episode 5, I was kind sad that
Ke Huy Quan’s character OB was basically

00:06:33.630 --> 00:06:46.480
just a character that rattled off complicated
exposition, but episode 5 fleshes him out

00:06:46.480 --> 00:06:48.820
and makes him more understandable as a character.

00:06:48.820 --> 00:06:53.000
And not just a vessel to explain different
mechanics within the world.

00:06:53.000 --> 00:06:56.919
Because he’s honestly just a downgraded
version from his character in Everything Everywhere

00:06:56.919 --> 00:07:01.620
All at Once, however, I think episode 5 luckily
redeems him.

00:07:01.620 --> 00:07:05.800
Sylvie I think has a good continuation and
her arc of slowly defusing from a destructionist

00:07:05.800 --> 00:07:08.470
mentality was really well done and explored.

00:07:08.470 --> 00:07:12.879
Her final scene with Loki was heartfelt and
perfectly punctuates the ending of her arc

00:07:12.879 --> 00:07:17.710
in aiding Loki in realizing that destroying
something is OK if you replace it with something

00:07:17.710 --> 00:07:18.750
better.

00:07:18.750 --> 00:07:22.879
Now for the last character I’ll address
that I don’t think was very well done was

00:07:22.879 --> 00:07:23.879
Renslayer.

00:07:23.879 --> 00:07:27.590
It feels like her villain arc was nowhere
near as complex as the other characters in

00:07:27.590 --> 00:07:28.590
the show.

00:07:28.590 --> 00:07:32.860
Because she essentially just wants power and
to control the TVA even though she’s literally

00:07:32.860 --> 00:07:37.260
causing the TVA’s downfall by delaying Mobius
and Loki in fixing the loom.

00:07:37.260 --> 00:07:40.860
I know she doesn’t trust them, but it feels
like a contradiction in that she wants to

00:07:40.860 --> 00:07:45.730
restore the TVA to it’s former glory while
also being the one that’s causing its downfall.

00:07:45.730 --> 00:07:50.599
I guess there is some irony there even though
it doesn’t feel very logical from a character

00:07:50.599 --> 00:07:51.889
point of view.

00:07:51.889 --> 00:07:56.100
Outside of her lust for power, there’s really
nothing else about her to make her fascinating.

00:07:56.100 --> 00:07:59.970
The revelation about her mind being wiped
I thought was a good twist for the character,

00:07:59.970 --> 00:08:02.949
but it again only feeds into that lust for
power.

00:08:02.949 --> 00:08:07.500
So if anything, it kind of feels like Renslayer
was used as a roadblock conflict in drawing

00:08:07.500 --> 00:08:12.419
out the story until the loom explodes because
without her the show would be much shorter.

00:08:12.419 --> 00:08:14.919
Which this is fine, I just wish she was more
compelling.

00:08:14.919 --> 00:08:19.389
Her state in the show was pretty underwhelming
considering how much screen time she had and

00:08:19.389 --> 00:08:22.110
especially how abrupt her ending was.

00:08:22.110 --> 00:08:26.070
The writers were kind of like, oh yeah and
by the way Renslayer dies after being sent

00:08:26.070 --> 00:08:27.870
to the void, that’s it.

00:08:27.870 --> 00:08:32.440
Then her and Miss Minutes’ lust towards
Victor Timely was very odd and off-putting

00:08:32.440 --> 00:08:34.680
to the point that it was not needed at all.

00:08:34.680 --> 00:08:38.880
So even though Renslayer was partially lacking,
I think Loki season 2’s characters were

00:08:38.880 --> 00:08:41.469
still outstanding all around.

00:08:41.469 --> 00:08:45.769
Another aspect that goes hand and hand with
the characters is the acting.

00:08:45.769 --> 00:08:50.060
Out of the entire MCU, I think Tom Hiddleston
is easily the best actor.

00:08:50.060 --> 00:08:55.111
The amount of range and raw emotion that is
constantly required for the character in every

00:08:55.111 --> 00:08:56.820
scene is flooring.

00:08:56.820 --> 00:09:00.960
It obviously helps that the writing allows
this, but there are so many beautiful close-ups

00:09:00.960 --> 00:09:05.589
of Loki throughout the season in which he
puts you in a trance with his amazing acting.

00:09:05.589 --> 00:09:11.329
Tom Hiddleston has a mastery understanding
of everything whether it being subtlety, gravitas,

00:09:11.329 --> 00:09:15.680
charisma, pacing, great delivery of lines,
and nails sorrow with ease.

00:09:15.680 --> 00:09:20.350
He’s literally a flawless actor and it’s
rare to be this consistently floored.

00:09:20.350 --> 00:09:24.360
I know that acting awards for the Emmys is
extremely crowded from how many great shows

00:09:24.360 --> 00:09:28.459
are running, but it would honestly feel like
a snub if Tom Hiddleston doesn’t get a Best

00:09:28.459 --> 00:09:29.620
Lead Actor nomination.

00:09:29.620 --> 00:09:34.410
Unfortunately, though, I feel like voters
will automatically look past this show just

00:09:34.410 --> 00:09:37.360
because of the Marvel brand not being what
it used to be.

00:09:37.360 --> 00:09:41.029
Without Tom Hiddleston, this show would be
practically nothing and he perfectly sells

00:09:41.029 --> 00:09:45.440
the anguish and turmoil Loki as a character
is constantly going through.

00:09:45.440 --> 00:09:49.700
Then other standouts I would say are definitely
Jonathan Majors and Sophia Martino.

00:09:49.700 --> 00:09:53.460
I know all the controversy around Jonathan
Majors is bad, but that doesn’t take away

00:09:53.460 --> 00:09:55.779
from the fact he did a great job in season
2.

00:09:55.779 --> 00:09:59.760
At first, I found Victor Timely to be kind
of annoying, but his wholesomeness quickly

00:09:59.760 --> 00:10:04.130
grew on me and I loved how Jonathan got to
revisit He Who Remains again.

00:10:04.130 --> 00:10:08.860
Sophia Martino like Tom Hiddleston delivers
deeply emotional moments with ease and they

00:10:08.860 --> 00:10:10.620
always make a great pairing.

00:10:10.620 --> 00:10:15.440
It’s just a little unfortunate she wasn’t
as heavily featured in season 2, but that’s

00:10:15.440 --> 00:10:18.029
fine since that’s what made sense for the
story.

00:10:18.029 --> 00:10:22.010
Then other actors who weren’t as prominent
in the story still did a great job.

00:10:22.010 --> 00:10:26.360
One of my favorite examples is the scene where
all the prisoners get killed and the filmmakers

00:10:26.360 --> 00:10:30.780
decide to only show the reactions of both
Wunmi and Rafael who perfectly mirror the

00:10:30.780 --> 00:10:32.519
horror they’re witnessing.

00:10:32.519 --> 00:10:37.320
Now I’ll go into one more great aspect about
Loki season 2 before going into some faults

00:10:37.320 --> 00:10:38.590
I had with the season.

00:10:38.590 --> 00:10:43.610
And the last great thing I want to dive into
is the production value of the show.

00:10:43.610 --> 00:10:48.370
Loki easily blows every other MCU production
out of the water because this is the only

00:10:48.370 --> 00:10:50.850
show that actually feels like a show.

00:10:50.850 --> 00:10:55.230
They’re constructing numerous real sets
that are re-used instead of constantly going

00:10:55.230 --> 00:10:59.481
to random locations to film scenes that are
in turn in dull locations because they’re

00:10:59.481 --> 00:11:01.360
only used once.

00:11:01.360 --> 00:11:05.200
Building these sets also probably saves them
quite a bit of money so that they can then

00:11:05.200 --> 00:11:08.160
spend it on making the CGI look great.

00:11:08.160 --> 00:11:12.089
Because throughout the show it looked pretty
spectacular and I especially loved the spaghetti

00:11:12.089 --> 00:11:14.900
unraveling of people being erased from time.

00:11:14.900 --> 00:11:17.380
It visually looked cool and was creative.

00:11:17.380 --> 00:11:22.140
The only CGI moment that was very odd was
actually the very beginning of season 2 where

00:11:22.140 --> 00:11:25.950
Loki jumps off of the TVA tower and lands
into a flying car.

00:11:25.950 --> 00:11:30.220
This shot immediately scared me from how rough
it looked, as if a beginner in blender made

00:11:30.220 --> 00:11:31.220
it, but luckily it gets way better.

00:11:31.220 --> 00:11:35.290
I would say that the moment right after this
where a practical car crashes into a real

00:11:35.290 --> 00:11:38.040
set redeems the janky-looking shot.

00:11:38.040 --> 00:11:42.500
But yeah, the overall visual look of the show
is of such a high quality and has a strong

00:11:42.500 --> 00:11:46.779
visual identity because of its unique and
exotic locations.

00:11:46.779 --> 00:11:51.380
Similar obviously to season 1, even though
I would argue that season 1 visually was more

00:11:51.380 --> 00:11:53.220
memorable than season 2.

00:11:53.220 --> 00:11:57.070
There is an entirely different cinematographer
and different directors that in turn make

00:11:57.070 --> 00:11:59.990
the show feel different visually, but I still
think it looks good.

00:11:59.990 --> 00:12:04.630
I don’t think the vast majority of viewers
will notice a major shift, although, to me,

00:12:04.630 --> 00:12:09.190
the first season looked a bit more dramatic
in terms of its lighting for different scenes.

00:12:09.190 --> 00:12:13.459
While season 2 feels more loose and is directed
and shot with an ensemble of characters taken

00:12:13.459 --> 00:12:15.300
into account.

00:12:15.300 --> 00:12:19.860
So going into what I think is the main fault
of the season, and I know this video is titled

00:12:19.860 --> 00:12:24.560
as being a triumph of a show, but I don’t
think season 2 is a flawless 10/10.

00:12:24.560 --> 00:12:29.139
The first half of the season in comparison
to the latter half I think is weaker and there

00:12:29.139 --> 00:12:30.889
are a few reasons for this.

00:12:30.889 --> 00:12:35.779
Firstly, the plot of episodes 2 and 3 were
primarily sub-plots that were there to buy

00:12:35.779 --> 00:12:37.860
time until the ending of the show.

00:12:37.860 --> 00:12:41.750
Now you may immediately disagree with that
assessment so let me explain.

00:12:41.750 --> 00:12:46.480
In episode 2, Loki and Mobius spend half of
the episode questioning Brad about where Sylvie

00:12:46.480 --> 00:12:51.050
is and also uncover a sudden conflict that
there are loyalists killing branching timelines.

00:12:51.050 --> 00:12:55.560
There’s not a lot of set-up for this development,
especially for Brad’s character.

00:12:55.560 --> 00:12:59.730
It feels as though we skipped over an episode
as this random background character becomes

00:12:59.730 --> 00:13:02.280
an integral centerpiece out of nowhere.

00:13:02.280 --> 00:13:06.019
So you spend half the time confused as to
if you missed something and why were spending

00:13:06.019 --> 00:13:07.780
so much time with him.

00:13:07.780 --> 00:13:11.810
Then after this conflict is resolved, the
leader of these loyalists becomes irrelevant

00:13:11.810 --> 00:13:15.060
within the story as Renslayer comes in to
kill her.

00:13:15.060 --> 00:13:18.850
Now is the writing for these scenes bad by
any means?

00:13:18.850 --> 00:13:19.850
Absolutely not.

00:13:19.850 --> 00:13:23.889
It just doesn’t feel like an efficient usage
of time in terms of storytelling.

00:13:23.889 --> 00:13:27.660
There is some importance for this episode
in that it ropes Sylvie back into the narrative,

00:13:27.660 --> 00:13:32.550
they’re able to find Renslayer and establishes
Brad as a character, but I can’t help but

00:13:32.550 --> 00:13:36.230
escape the feeling that we're just spending
time on a sub-plot for this episode.

00:13:36.230 --> 00:13:39.190
And the same thing applies for episode 3.

00:13:39.190 --> 00:13:43.480
I know Victor Timely is a pretty important
character, but the writers only make him that

00:13:43.480 --> 00:13:48.060
way because they randomly require the creator’s
aura to unlock the blast doors.

00:13:48.060 --> 00:13:52.980
The writers only do this to give Victor Timely
importance within the narrative because, besides

00:13:52.980 --> 00:13:55.630
that, he doesn’t really offer anything to
the story.

00:13:55.630 --> 00:13:59.970
He helps out with some scientific problem-solving,
but that’s kind of a minor point that you

00:13:59.970 --> 00:14:02.170
can just write to have OB think of.

00:14:02.170 --> 00:14:07.100
So for episode 3, we essentially have this
entire artificial side-quest to get a character

00:14:07.100 --> 00:14:11.089
who has random importance because the writers
willed it to happen.

00:14:11.089 --> 00:14:14.850
Now again, the time spent here in the show
doesn’t mean it was bad.

00:14:14.850 --> 00:14:19.620
Just in comparison to the latter half of season
2 which features a clean and efficient narrative,

00:14:19.620 --> 00:14:21.779
the first half feels inferior.

00:14:21.779 --> 00:14:26.910
The writers still include solid character
development and overall well-written scenes,

00:14:26.910 --> 00:14:29.259
so I think it partially alleviates this fault.

00:14:29.259 --> 00:14:33.100
Also, I think it could have been a narrative
upgrade if they had Mobius be the one who

00:14:33.100 --> 00:14:37.300
goes out on the catwalk in episodes 4 and
6 instead of Victor.

00:14:37.300 --> 00:14:42.000
Mobius’ loss at the end of episode 4 would
be much more of a motivator for Loki because

00:14:42.000 --> 00:14:44.089
Mobius is like his best friend.

00:14:44.089 --> 00:14:48.509
Along with having much higher stakes because
we as an audience care more about Mobius rather

00:14:48.509 --> 00:14:50.860
than a character who just showed up.

00:14:50.860 --> 00:14:55.130
That and imagine how much more of a gut-punch
the ending to episode 4 would be if we thought

00:14:55.130 --> 00:14:56.779
Mobius died.

00:14:56.779 --> 00:15:01.790
So in an overall sense, I don’t think Victor
Timely ultimately added a lot to the story

00:15:01.790 --> 00:15:04.760
and could have been removed from it without
much loss being felt.

00:15:04.760 --> 00:15:08.550
And I feel like I have to keep bringing this
up in which I’m not knocking the quality

00:15:08.550 --> 00:15:13.240
of episodes 2 and 3 and just the relevance
and importance to the main plot.

00:15:13.240 --> 00:15:17.029
Since there are only 6 episodes within the
season, spending time on subplots is felt

00:15:17.029 --> 00:15:20.290
even more because the story is already short.

00:15:20.290 --> 00:15:24.730
This in comparison to season 1, where every
episode was extremely productive in progressing

00:15:24.730 --> 00:15:28.130
the main story, starts to feel like a stark
contrast.

00:15:28.130 --> 00:15:32.360
Which speaking of this comparison, I think
season 1 of Loki is a bit stronger because

00:15:32.360 --> 00:15:36.170
of the fact that each episode thoroughly engages
you from a plot perspective.

00:15:36.170 --> 00:15:41.380
What I mean by this is that every episode
hooks you with a captivating mystery as we

00:15:41.380 --> 00:15:44.910
keep going further down this alluring rabbit
hole of a story.

00:15:44.910 --> 00:15:49.230
Like figuring out what the TVA is, finding
out that we are hunting a variant whose a

00:15:49.230 --> 00:15:53.790
Loki, the Loki variant being a girl and her
actually being kind of a protagonist, the

00:15:53.790 --> 00:15:58.569
timekeepers being fake, overcoming the void
and Goliath, and meeting He Who Remains and

00:15:58.569 --> 00:16:01.240
getting answers to all of our questions.

00:16:01.240 --> 00:16:05.790
Season 1 is utterly packed to the brim with
an engaging and fun plot that it kind of makes

00:16:05.790 --> 00:16:08.880
season 2 seem underwhelming in comparison.

00:16:08.880 --> 00:16:13.110
Where in season 2 the plot is basically just
trying to fix the loom with a couple subplots

00:16:13.110 --> 00:16:15.339
interjected to make the season longer.

00:16:15.339 --> 00:16:18.750
I wouldn’t say this is very bad because
the narrative still hits an absolute high

00:16:18.750 --> 00:16:20.510
point for the finale.

00:16:20.510 --> 00:16:23.410
Just it’s kind of a shame that the first
half of the season doesn’t really match

00:16:23.410 --> 00:16:26.290
the high quality of the second half of the
season.

00:16:26.290 --> 00:16:30.370
So that’s really the only main flaw I had
with season 2 that brought it down slightly

00:16:30.370 --> 00:16:33.970
and there were also occasionally some weird
moments in terms of writing.

00:16:33.970 --> 00:16:38.389
Like the surreal scene where Victor insists
upon going to get a cup of coffee where Brad

00:16:38.389 --> 00:16:40.610
kills Soap and kidnaps Victor.

00:16:40.610 --> 00:16:44.780
That was honestly one of the weirdest and
most contrived scenes in the entire show that

00:16:44.780 --> 00:16:48.670
it felt jarring watching that scene in an
otherwise great episode.

00:16:48.670 --> 00:16:52.430
Then another contrived moment that stood out
to me was Sylvie sending Renslayer to the

00:16:52.430 --> 00:16:54.360
end of time instead of killing her.

00:16:54.360 --> 00:16:58.069
Like she didn’t even take her tempad and
I thought she was intending her being stuck

00:16:58.069 --> 00:16:59.940
there as a fate being worse than death.

00:16:59.940 --> 00:17:04.510
But yeah, every now and then the writing falters
and the tone can feel a bit weird at times

00:17:04.510 --> 00:17:08.520
like Victor’s chase scene in episode 3,
but I think the average quality of season

00:17:08.520 --> 00:17:13.059
2 is high enough to the point that these sudden
quality shifts aren’t a major deal.

00:17:13.059 --> 00:17:17.170
That and the ending is so amazing that you
can forgive some of the show’s faults.

00:17:17.170 --> 00:17:22.030
So overall, Loki season 2 even though I’d
say is a minor downgrade from season 1, is

00:17:22.030 --> 00:17:27.179
still far and away better than basically anything
Marvel has produced in the last few years.

00:17:27.179 --> 00:17:30.690
This is the bar of quality that I wish the
MCU can regularly operate in.

00:17:30.690 --> 00:17:35.190
It’s kind of unfortunate that Disney is
also releasing The Marvels at the same time

00:17:35.190 --> 00:17:37.549
as the finale of Loki season 2.

00:17:37.549 --> 00:17:42.150
So they’re essentially overshadowing something
they made that’s great, with something that’s

00:17:42.150 --> 00:17:43.150
bad.

00:17:43.150 --> 00:17:45.190
That just seems baffling to me that they would
do that.

00:17:45.190 --> 00:17:46.240
But thank you for watching.

00:17:46.240 --> 00:17:50.360
My Letterboxd, Patreon, and Discord links
are in the description, and I also just did

00:17:50.360 --> 00:17:52.780
a review on The Marvels if you want to check
that out too.

